{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"MPSimmons"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"A couple of years ago, I left aerospace (SpaceX then Varda) to found a weather data company called Local Weather News Network (LWNN).<p>Today, we're <em>launchin</em>g our first commercial offering, targeted at outdoor contractors who need to know the weather and site conditions before they go. What most site managers do for landscaping and roofing is just to check their phone's weather app for the various locations they need to dispatch crews, and try to figure out if any jobs should be cancelled or postponed. One owner told me he wakes up at 4am every day, because if he lets them know after 6am, he has to pay them even if they can't work.<p>Prelay monitors the entire upcoming schedule of sites and determine suitability of 62 activities across 12 trades. We're using ensemble models for weather, and a custom ground-wetness model for soil conditions. Users can also configure weather-triggered notifications so they reschedule work as early as possible.<p>As of today, Prelay is available in the US, and people can check it out at <a href=\"https://prelay.lwnn.news\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://prelay.lwnn.news</a> - I'm super interested in hearing from anyone who works in weather-sensitive businesses or has built similar software in this (or another) niche.<p>One question we've gone back and forth on is where the line should be drawn between providing raw weather data and recommending concrete actions (something like, 'there's a 35% chance of rain' vs 'you should consider postponing this job'). I'm curious what HN thinks of that question, and what you'd suggest."},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Prelay \u2013 Weather-based work planning for outdoor contractors"}},"_tags":["story","author_MPSimmons","story_50341158","show_hn"],"author":"MPSimmons","created_at":"2026-11-02T18:20:46Z","created_at_i":1793643646,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50341158","points":2,"story_id":50341158,"story_text":"A couple of years ago, I left aerospace (SpaceX then Varda) to found a weather data company called Local Weather News Network (LWNN).<p>Today, we&#x27;re launching our first commercial offering, targeted at outdoor contractors who need to know the weather and site conditions before they go. What most site managers do for landscaping and roofing is just to check their phone&#x27;s weather app for the various locations they need to dispatch crews, and try to figure out if any jobs should be cancelled or postponed. One owner told me he wakes up at 4am every day, because if he lets them know after 6am, he has to pay them even if they can&#x27;t work.<p>Prelay monitors the entire upcoming schedule of sites and determine suitability of 62 activities across 12 trades. We&#x27;re using ensemble models for weather, and a custom ground-wetness model for soil conditions. Users can also configure weather-triggered notifications so they reschedule work as early as possible.<p>As of today, Prelay is available in the US, and people can check it out at <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;prelay.lwnn.news\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;prelay.lwnn.news</a> - I&#x27;m super interested in hearing from anyone who works in weather-sensitive businesses or has built similar software in this (or another) niche.<p>One question we&#x27;ve gone back and forth on is where the line should be drawn between providing raw weather data and recommending concrete actions (something like, &#x27;there&#x27;s a 35% chance of rain&#x27; vs &#x27;you should consider postponing this job&#x27;). I&#x27;m curious what HN thinks of that question, and what you&#x27;d suggest.","title":"Show HN: Prelay \u2013 Weather-based work planning for outdoor contractors","updated_at":"2026-11-02T18:23:58Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"NickySlicks"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"I got tired of getting served strange and weird ads due to the fact that our TVs monitor just about everything we do. So I built an HDMI man in the middle to detect ads and replace them.<p>Presented the concept and <em>launched</em> the open source repo this year at HOPE 2026, huge thanks for the conference for giving me a chance to spread this tech.<p>Link to the repo on the video"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Spy Back on Your TV: HDMI Man in the Middle"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ktMCGZ_xHi4"}},"_tags":["story","author_NickySlicks","story_50338144","show_hn"],"author":"NickySlicks","created_at":"2026-11-02T15:27:20Z","created_at_i":1793633240,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50338144","points":3,"story_id":50338144,"story_text":"I got tired of getting served strange and weird ads due to the fact that our TVs monitor just about everything we do. So I built an HDMI man in the middle to detect ads and replace them.<p>Presented the concept and launched the open source repo this year at HOPE 2026, huge thanks for the conference for giving me a chance to spread this tech.<p>Link to the repo on the video","title":"Show HN: Spy Back on Your TV: HDMI Man in the Middle","updated_at":"2026-11-02T22:04:43Z","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ktMCGZ_xHi4"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"ku5h"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"Hi HN, I am Kush, a student founder from India building mxRaven (<a href=\"https://mxraven.com\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://mxraven.com</a>). We recently made it available for public use, you can read my post about our <em>launch</em> here: <a href=\"https://mxraven.com/blog/launching-mxraven\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://mxraven.com/blog/<em>launch</em>ing-mxraven</a><p>mxRaven is a programmable email delivery platform for not any one particular thing, but rather a flexible glue which can be used to build anything that requires receiving or sending emails in some form, be it agent mailboxes, personal mailboxes, transactional email, marketing campaigns etc. Think of it as a sort of WAF + Application Layer Router but for the SMTP world with ergonomic SDKs for keeping the developers in the driver's seat.<p>For this public demo (<a href=\"https://sandbox.mxraven.com/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://sandbox.mxraven.com/</a>), I vibecoded a sandbox that (tries) to preview as many mxRaven features as possible publicly without possibility of abuse. You can try to send or receive emails from and to your designated sandbox addresses and play with some basic subset of programmable rules (<a href=\"https://mxraven.com/docs/concepts/routing-and-actions/introduction/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://mxraven.com/docs/concepts/routing-and-actions/introd...</a>)<p>You can read the details about mxRaven from the docs <a href=\"https://mxraven.com/docs/concepts/start-here/why-mxraven/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://mxraven.com/docs/concepts/start-here/why-mxraven/</a> and check out our first-party SDKs we have so far:<p><a href=\"https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-js\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-js</a>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-go\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-go</a>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-py\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-py</a>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-java\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/synqronlabs/mxraven-java</a><p>P.S. Unlike some of our competition we do not use relays like AWS SES. In fact mxRaven was born from the open source raven library (<a href=\"https://github.com/synqronlabs/raven\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/synqronlabs/raven</a>) a high performance comprehensive ESMTP toolkit I wrote in Go. We ARE the email relay, and even the core transport and email auth library is first party and open source, something I am very proud of."},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: MxRaven \u2013 Programmable email infra for nothing in particular"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://sandbox.mxraven.com/"}},"_tags":["story","author_ku5h","story_50338055","show_hn"],"author":"ku5h","children":[50338119,50338326],"created_at":"2026-11-02T15:22:49Z","created_at_i":1793632969,"num_comments":2,"objectID":"50338055","points":3,"story_id":50338055,"story_text":"Hi HN, I am Kush, a student founder from India building mxRaven (<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com</a>). We recently made it available for public use, you can read my post about our launch here: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;launching-mxraven\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;launching-mxraven</a><p>mxRaven is a programmable email delivery platform for not any one particular thing, but rather a flexible glue which can be used to build anything that requires receiving or sending emails in some form, be it agent mailboxes, personal mailboxes, transactional email, marketing campaigns etc. Think of it as a sort of WAF + Application Layer Router but for the SMTP world with ergonomic SDKs for keeping the developers in the driver&#x27;s seat.<p>For this public demo (<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;sandbox.mxraven.com&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;sandbox.mxraven.com&#x2F;</a>), I vibecoded a sandbox that (tries) to preview as many mxRaven features as possible publicly without possibility of abuse. You can try to send or receive emails from and to your designated sandbox addresses and play with some basic subset of programmable rules (<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;concepts&#x2F;routing-and-actions&#x2F;introduction&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;concepts&#x2F;routing-and-actions&#x2F;introd...</a>)<p>You can read the details about mxRaven from the docs <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;concepts&#x2F;start-here&#x2F;why-mxraven&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mxraven.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;concepts&#x2F;start-here&#x2F;why-mxraven&#x2F;</a> and check out our first-party SDKs we have so far:<p><a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-js\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-js</a>\n<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-go\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-go</a>\n<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-py\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-py</a>\n<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-java\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;mxraven-java</a><p>P.S. Unlike some of our competition we do not use relays like AWS SES. In fact mxRaven was born from the open source raven library (<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;raven\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;synqronlabs&#x2F;raven</a>) a high performance comprehensive ESMTP toolkit I wrote in Go. We ARE the email relay, and even the core transport and email auth library is first party and open source, something I am very proud of.","title":"Show HN: MxRaven \u2013 Programmable email infra for nothing in particular","updated_at":"2026-11-02T16:52:45Z","url":"https://sandbox.mxraven.com/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"adamontherun"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"<em>Launched</em> <a href=\"https://better-together.fun\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://better-together.fun</a> this weekend to allow our personal AI agents to collaborate on problems that are tough to solve solo.<p>First up is dealing with spam texts and calls."},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Better Together \u2013 AI agents team up against spam calls and texts"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://better-together.fun/"}},"_tags":["story","author_adamontherun","story_50331415","show_hn"],"author":"adamontherun","created_at":"2026-11-02T00:19:11Z","created_at_i":1793578751,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50331415","points":6,"story_id":50331415,"story_text":"Launched <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;better-together.fun\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;better-together.fun</a> this weekend to allow our personal AI agents to collaborate on problems that are tough to solve solo.<p>First up is dealing with spam texts and calls.","title":"Show HN: Better Together \u2013 AI agents team up against spam calls and texts","updated_at":"2026-11-02T17:01:43Z","url":"https://better-together.fun/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"calvinyz"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"A few days ago, I got OpenAI's email that Prism will be officially shutting down on January 1. Having written my last paper in it, I was quite disappointed since I've never really enjoyed typing LaTeX by hand for long periods. Also, when I updated my macOS recently, I started playing around with Apple's Foundation Models a bit (which I'm late on). So on Saturday I decided that, instead of studying for my midterms, my weekend project was going to be building a local version of Prism that used on-device Apple FMs.<p>It's an Overleaf-style editor with a PDF preview that compiles with TeX Live, and it installs TeX for you if you don't have it. To use the AI features, select text and press Cmd-K, and you can accept or reject the diff. If you have a handwritten formula that you want to insert, you can place your cursor where you want to insert and drop in a photo, which GLM-OCR turns into LaTeX locally since Apple's OCR doesn't handle math.<p>What was fun was testing out the capability of the models. Corollary uses SystemLanguageModel.default, which is Apple's general-purpose on-device model and the one built into macOS for Apple Intelligence. It's about 3B params with a 4,096 token context window on my M1 Max. When asked for plain text, it kept every \\cite, \\ref, \\label and formula intact in 78 of 80 edits, but with structured output (@Generable) it went 0 for 20 because the JSON escaping eats the backslashes (\\text comes back as a tab). While this limits capabilities like full document edits, you can already point it at Ollama or your own API key, and I'm sure the models will catch up soon.<p>Also, it needs an Apple silicon Mac, and the AI needs macOS 26 with Apple Intelligence. At this stage I don't have an Apple Developer ID yet, so the first <em>launch</em> needs System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Open Anyway. Sorry about that. Please give it a try and let me know what bugs you run into or what the model struggles on!<p>Github: <a href=\"https://github.com/calvinyz/corollary\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/calvinyz/corollary</a>"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Corollary, LaTeX Editor for Mac with On-Device Apple Foundation Models"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://calvinyz.github.io/corollary/"}},"_tags":["story","author_calvinyz","story_50330224","show_hn"],"author":"calvinyz","created_at":"2026-11-01T21:45:36Z","created_at_i":1793569536,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50330224","points":2,"story_id":50330224,"story_text":"A few days ago, I got OpenAI&#x27;s email that Prism will be officially shutting down on January 1. Having written my last paper in it, I was quite disappointed since I&#x27;ve never really enjoyed typing LaTeX by hand for long periods. Also, when I updated my macOS recently, I started playing around with Apple&#x27;s Foundation Models a bit (which I&#x27;m late on). So on Saturday I decided that, instead of studying for my midterms, my weekend project was going to be building a local version of Prism that used on-device Apple FMs.<p>It&#x27;s an Overleaf-style editor with a PDF preview that compiles with TeX Live, and it installs TeX for you if you don&#x27;t have it. To use the AI features, select text and press Cmd-K, and you can accept or reject the diff. If you have a handwritten formula that you want to insert, you can place your cursor where you want to insert and drop in a photo, which GLM-OCR turns into LaTeX locally since Apple&#x27;s OCR doesn&#x27;t handle math.<p>What was fun was testing out the capability of the models. Corollary uses SystemLanguageModel.default, which is Apple&#x27;s general-purpose on-device model and the one built into macOS for Apple Intelligence. It&#x27;s about 3B params with a 4,096 token context window on my M1 Max. When asked for plain text, it kept every \\cite, \\ref, \\label and formula intact in 78 of 80 edits, but with structured output (@Generable) it went 0 for 20 because the JSON escaping eats the backslashes (\\text comes back as a tab). While this limits capabilities like full document edits, you can already point it at Ollama or your own API key, and I&#x27;m sure the models will catch up soon.<p>Also, it needs an Apple silicon Mac, and the AI needs macOS 26 with Apple Intelligence. At this stage I don&#x27;t have an Apple Developer ID yet, so the first launch needs System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Open Anyway. Sorry about that. Please give it a try and let me know what bugs you run into or what the model struggles on!<p>Github: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;calvinyz&#x2F;corollary\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;calvinyz&#x2F;corollary</a>","title":"Show HN: Corollary, LaTeX Editor for Mac with On-Device Apple Foundation Models","updated_at":"2026-11-02T06:10:25Z","url":"https://calvinyz.github.io/corollary/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"egz"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"We're <em>launchin</em>g Maintainers Coffee to make mailing a small thanks to someone with appreciated work more simple.<p>Connect a GitHub account and add shipping details to receive orders at a unique link as: <a href=\"https://maintainers.coffee/github/theorderingmachine\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://maintainers.coffee/github/theorderingmachine</a><p>Someone can send coffee for USD $8 to either Canada or the US.<p>Check out how this appears alongside GitHub Sponsors: <a href=\"https://github.com/maintainersdotcoffee/FUNDING.yml\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/maintainersdotcoffee/FUNDING.yml</a><p>Feedback on sending and receiving flows and personal comfort creating and sharing a link is so welcomed!"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Maintainers Coffee \u2013 Ship coffee to someone making something cool"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://maintainers.coffee/"}},"_tags":["story","author_egz","story_50330154","show_hn"],"author":"egz","children":[50330296],"created_at":"2026-11-01T21:36:19Z","created_at_i":1793568979,"num_comments":2,"objectID":"50330154","points":1,"story_id":50330154,"story_text":"We&#x27;re launching Maintainers Coffee to make mailing a small thanks to someone with appreciated work more simple.<p>Connect a GitHub account and add shipping details to receive orders at a unique link as: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;maintainers.coffee&#x2F;github&#x2F;theorderingmachine\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;maintainers.coffee&#x2F;github&#x2F;theorderingmachine</a><p>Someone can send coffee for USD $8 to either Canada or the US.<p>Check out how this appears alongside GitHub Sponsors: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;maintainersdotcoffee&#x2F;FUNDING.yml\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;maintainersdotcoffee&#x2F;FUNDING.yml</a><p>Feedback on sending and receiving flows and personal comfort creating and sharing a link is so welcomed!","title":"Show HN: Maintainers Coffee \u2013 Ship coffee to someone making something cool","updated_at":"2026-11-02T00:38:42Z","url":"https://maintainers.coffee/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"stronxyo"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"Quarter sits on top of the <em>launcher</em> client you already have. It grabs your library and hands every action back to the <em>launcher</em>, there's no login or password, no API key or data collection. Your client does everything else.<p>Quarter is just a fast shortcut to play your favorite games from the terminal you live in every day. That was my main mission when I created Quarter; just insert quarter, pick a game, press Enter.<p>Written in Go with Bubble Tea, reads Steam's own files on disk; game logos are drawn as pictures in the terminal using Sixel (terminals that don't support it will have a text fallback).<p>Steam today; Epic, GOG, EA and more <em>launcher</em>s next.<p>Tested on Windows 11 with Windows Terminal. Did automated checks on Linux and Mac; but no tests as a real human with Steam installed, I appreciate the feedback a lot.<p>What <em>launcher</em> would you like next?"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"Show HN: Quarter \u2013 A TUI <em>launcher</em> to browse, search and launch your games"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://github.com/stronxyo/quarter"}},"_tags":["story","author_stronxyo","story_50329627","show_hn"],"author":"stronxyo","children":[50334965],"created_at":"2026-11-01T20:40:43Z","created_at_i":1793565643,"num_comments":2,"objectID":"50329627","points":5,"story_id":50329627,"story_text":"Quarter sits on top of the launcher client you already have. It grabs your library and hands every action back to the launcher, there&#x27;s no login or password, no API key or data collection. Your client does everything else.<p>Quarter is just a fast shortcut to play your favorite games from the terminal you live in every day. That was my main mission when I created Quarter; just insert quarter, pick a game, press Enter.<p>Written in Go with Bubble Tea, reads Steam&#x27;s own files on disk; game logos are drawn as pictures in the terminal using Sixel (terminals that don&#x27;t support it will have a text fallback).<p>Steam today; Epic, GOG, EA and more launchers next.<p>Tested on Windows 11 with Windows Terminal. Did automated checks on Linux and Mac; but no tests as a real human with Steam installed, I appreciate the feedback a lot.<p>What launcher would you like next?","title":"Show HN: Quarter \u2013 A TUI launcher to browse, search and launch your games","updated_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:28Z","url":"https://github.com/stronxyo/quarter"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"josephspurrier"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"I usually have many projects and terminals I\u2019m working in so Crosswalk groups my work together and allows me to open a terminal (or another terminal app) in the correct folder/project quickly and label the tab (as well as add notes). I started on iTerm2 (which is great) years ago for my terminal manager, then found I spent too much time flipping between tabs once I started leveraging terminal agents like Claude Code and Codex so I built Crosswalk to improve visibility at a glance. It originally started as a web page, but then I moved it to an app so I had better control over notifications and sounds to let me know when work was done or needed me on one of the tabs.<p>Crosswalk shows which sessions are still working or sitting idle with an activity dot on the tab. It provides hotkeys to jump between tabs and groups quickly. Even if you're not using agents, you can still use it for your terminal work - I commonly leave active projects as groups on the left even if I not working in them right now. There's an MCP server so an agent can <em>launch</em> new tabs (and agents) - it's helpful when you need a lead agent to spawn long running agents (in visible tabs) and then monitor their status (running, stuck, needs user feedback) or compact their context at efficient times. There are other nice features like layouts to view multiple terminals at once and reopens terminals from last session. I spent a bunch of time iterating on the pty host (pseudoterminal host) so updating the app or closing and then reopening will restore the sessions.<p>This is a paid product you can trial for free. I created it to solve the multi agent bottlenecks so I use it daily, both personally and professionally. For Mac and Windows - Electron based."},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Crosswalk \u2013 terminal manager to reduce sprawl"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://wareshq.com/crosswalk"}},"_tags":["story","author_josephspurrier","story_50328826","show_hn"],"author":"josephspurrier","created_at":"2026-11-01T19:28:49Z","created_at_i":1793561329,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50328826","points":1,"story_id":50328826,"story_text":"I usually have many projects and terminals I\u2019m working in so Crosswalk groups my work together and allows me to open a terminal (or another terminal app) in the correct folder&#x2F;project quickly and label the tab (as well as add notes). I started on iTerm2 (which is great) years ago for my terminal manager, then found I spent too much time flipping between tabs once I started leveraging terminal agents like Claude Code and Codex so I built Crosswalk to improve visibility at a glance. It originally started as a web page, but then I moved it to an app so I had better control over notifications and sounds to let me know when work was done or needed me on one of the tabs.<p>Crosswalk shows which sessions are still working or sitting idle with an activity dot on the tab. It provides hotkeys to jump between tabs and groups quickly. Even if you&#x27;re not using agents, you can still use it for your terminal work - I commonly leave active projects as groups on the left even if I not working in them right now. There&#x27;s an MCP server so an agent can launch new tabs (and agents) - it&#x27;s helpful when you need a lead agent to spawn long running agents (in visible tabs) and then monitor their status (running, stuck, needs user feedback) or compact their context at efficient times. There are other nice features like layouts to view multiple terminals at once and reopens terminals from last session. I spent a bunch of time iterating on the pty host (pseudoterminal host) so updating the app or closing and then reopening will restore the sessions.<p>This is a paid product you can trial for free. I created it to solve the multi agent bottlenecks so I use it daily, both personally and professionally. For Mac and Windows - Electron based.","title":"Show HN: Crosswalk \u2013 terminal manager to reduce sprawl","updated_at":"2026-11-01T19:34:41Z","url":"https://wareshq.com/crosswalk"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"popcar2"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"Hi HN! I just released a new minimalist website that lets you play over 150 PICO 8 games on the web that I curated myself. It's fully open source and made from the ground up to be tiny and fast, allowing you to switch games instantly by hotswapping them into the game player.<p>The website also works as a PWA that works on mobile and can run offline after <em>launchin</em>g it once! The <i>entire</i> website with all 150+ games can be downloaded in under 4 megabytes of internet use. Any feedback or questions are welcome. Enjoy!<p>GitHub Repo: <a href=\"https://github.com/popcar2/PicoPlayer\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/popcar2/PicoPlayer</a>\nTechnical details on how it works: <a href=\"https://picoplayer.net/technical_deets\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://picoplayer.net/technical_deets</a>"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: PicoPlayer \u2013 A tiny, open-source website for playing quality webgames"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://picoplayer.net/"}},"_tags":["story","author_popcar2","story_50303455","show_hn"],"author":"popcar2","children":[50305546,50309724,50307682,50327236],"created_at":"2026-10-30T13:17:45Z","created_at_i":1793366265,"num_comments":9,"objectID":"50303455","points":13,"story_id":50303455,"story_text":"Hi HN! I just released a new minimalist website that lets you play over 150 PICO 8 games on the web that I curated myself. It&#x27;s fully open source and made from the ground up to be tiny and fast, allowing you to switch games instantly by hotswapping them into the game player.<p>The website also works as a PWA that works on mobile and can run offline after launching it once! The <i>entire</i> website with all 150+ games can be downloaded in under 4 megabytes of internet use. Any feedback or questions are welcome. Enjoy!<p>GitHub Repo: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;popcar2&#x2F;PicoPlayer\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;popcar2&#x2F;PicoPlayer</a>\nTechnical details on how it works: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;picoplayer.net&#x2F;technical_deets\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;picoplayer.net&#x2F;technical_deets</a>","title":"Show HN: PicoPlayer \u2013 A tiny, open-source website for playing quality webgames","updated_at":"2026-11-03T00:38:45Z","url":"https://picoplayer.net/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"darindd"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["launched"],"value":"Hey HN, I'm Darin. You might know me from my work at Webflow (YC S13) and currently Ploy (YC P26).<p>Sunlight is the way I imagined the Mac working before I got my 1st-gen MacBook Air in high school (2008). I had looked at a bunch of Apple videos of Mission Control (Expos\u00e9 back then) and somehow decided it worked like an infinite canvas. I even told my mom about it! I guess it stuck with me.<p>Windowing on the Mac is so good it's one of those &quot;if it ain't broke don't fix it&quot; things. And yet, it always felt lacking. So Sunlight reimagines it from first principles:<p>- 355&quot;+ virtual desktop<p>- 120fps, fully native<p>- Multi-touch interactions<p>- Guides, grids and haptics<p>- Minimap<p>- \u2318Tab and Mission Control work as usual<p>- Works with your Dock, widgets and desktop<p>- Collision detection and magnetic windows<p>- Tiling and saved layouts<p>- Deeply customizable<p>- ...and much more (including agent support) coming soon<p>Free and open source, forever.<p><em>Launch</em> demo (2:34): <a href=\"https://youtu.be/NSerIT8zRpw\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://youtu.be/NSerIT8zRpw</a><p>Repo: <a href=\"https://github.com/spacefarm-digital/sunlight\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/spacefarm-digital/sunlight</a>"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Sunlight \u2013 the camera-based infinite canvas window manager for Mac"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://sunlight.software/"}},"_tags":["story","author_darindd","story_50295178","show_hn"],"author":"darindd","children":[50300511,50318408],"created_at":"2026-10-29T20:15:28Z","created_at_i":1793304928,"num_comments":2,"objectID":"50295178","points":7,"story_id":50295178,"story_text":"Hey HN, I&#x27;m Darin. You might know me from my work at Webflow (YC S13) and currently Ploy (YC P26).<p>Sunlight is the way I imagined the Mac working before I got my 1st-gen MacBook Air in high school (2008). I had looked at a bunch of Apple videos of Mission Control (Expos\u00e9 back then) and somehow decided it worked like an infinite canvas. I even told my mom about it! I guess it stuck with me.<p>Windowing on the Mac is so good it&#x27;s one of those &quot;if it ain&#x27;t broke don&#x27;t fix it&quot; things. And yet, it always felt lacking. So Sunlight reimagines it from first principles:<p>- 355&quot;+ virtual desktop<p>- 120fps, fully native<p>- Multi-touch interactions<p>- Guides, grids and haptics<p>- Minimap<p>- \u2318Tab and Mission Control work as usual<p>- Works with your Dock, widgets and desktop<p>- Collision detection and magnetic windows<p>- Tiling and saved layouts<p>- Deeply customizable<p>- ...and much more (including agent support) coming soon<p>Free and open source, forever.<p>Launch demo (2:34): <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;youtu.be&#x2F;NSerIT8zRpw\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;youtu.be&#x2F;NSerIT8zRpw</a><p>Repo: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;spacefarm-digital&#x2F;sunlight\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;spacefarm-digital&#x2F;sunlight</a>","title":"Show HN: Sunlight \u2013 the camera-based infinite canvas window manager for Mac","updated_at":"2026-10-31T18:04:06Z","url":"https://sunlight.software/"}],"hitsPerPage":10,"nbHits":66,"nbPages":7,"page":0,"params":"query=launched&tags=show_hn&hitsPerPage=10&page=0&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E%3D1791084783%2Ccreated_at_i%3C%3D1793676783&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":9,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"roundTrip":20},"afterFetch":{"format":{"highlighting":1,"total":1}},"fetch":{"query":7,"total":8},"total":9},"query":"launched","serverTimeMS":10}
